1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,172
Last year, Americans told the FBI they lost

2
00:00:03,172 --> 00:00:06,045
twenty point nine billion dollars to online

3
00:00:06,045 --> 00:00:08,918
crime. That's more than fifty-seven million

4
00:00:08,918 --> 00:00:10,615
dollars, every single day.

5
00:00:11,155 --> 00:00:13,616
But most of that money went to just five

6
00:00:13,616 --> 00:00:16,016
scams. Today, we're counting them down,

7
00:00:16,016 --> 00:00:17,876
from number five to number one.

8
00:00:18,416 --> 00:00:20,791
Number one took more than eight billion

9
00:00:20,791 --> 00:00:23,225
dollars. And it almost never starts with

10
00:00:23,225 --> 00:00:24,412
a request for money.

11
00:00:24,952 --> 00:00:27,199
Stay until the end, because there's one

12
00:00:27,199 --> 00:00:29,446
simple rule that would have stopped all

13
00:00:29,446 --> 00:00:29,727
five.

14
00:00:30,267 --> 00:00:33,869
All numbers are from the FBI's 2025 Internet

15
00:00:33,869 --> 00:00:34,990
Crime Report.

16
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:38,273
The stories are composites based on real

17
00:00:38,273 --> 00:00:38,753
cases.

18
00:00:39,593 --> 00:00:42,067
Number five: government impersonation.

19
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:44,859
Seven hundred ninety-eight million dollars,

20
00:00:44,859 --> 00:00:47,714
and more than thirty-two thousand complaints.

21
00:00:48,254 --> 00:00:49,888
Dolores is seventy-one.

22
00:00:49,888 --> 00:00:52,407
Her phone shows a government number.

23
00:00:52,407 --> 00:00:55,538
A stern voice says her Social Security number

24
00:00:55,538 --> 00:00:58,466
was used in a crime, and her benefits will

25
00:00:58,466 --> 00:00:59,555
be frozen today.

26
00:01:00,095 --> 00:01:02,665
To 'protect' her savings, he tells her to

27
00:01:02,665 --> 00:01:05,113
move the money to a 'safe account.' And

28
00:01:05,113 --> 00:01:07,561
not to tell anyone, because the case is

29
00:01:07,561 --> 00:01:08,357
confidential.

30
00:01:08,897 --> 00:01:11,157
The trick is fear plus authority.

31
00:01:11,157 --> 00:01:14,016
And the caller ID can be faked to show any

32
00:01:14,016 --> 00:01:14,947
agency's name.

33
00:01:15,487 --> 00:01:18,541
Scammers pretend to be the IRS, Medicare,

34
00:01:18,541 --> 00:01:20,431
the police, even the FBI.

35
00:01:20,431 --> 00:01:23,339
Some send photos of fake badges or fake

36
00:01:23,339 --> 00:01:24,575
arrest warrants.

37
00:01:24,575 --> 00:01:27,773
The goal is always the same: make you panic

38
00:01:27,773 --> 00:01:29,300
before you can think.

39
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:32,528
The red flag: real agencies don't call to

40
00:01:32,528 --> 00:01:35,089
threaten you, and they never ask you to

41
00:01:35,089 --> 00:01:36,753
move money to protect it.

42
00:01:36,753 --> 00:01:39,441
Hang up, and call the agency yourself, on

43
00:01:39,441 --> 00:01:41,809
the number from its official website.

44
00:01:42,349 --> 00:01:44,543
Dolores almost moved her savings.

45
00:01:44,543 --> 00:01:47,252
What stopped her was a sticky note on her

46
00:01:47,252 --> 00:01:50,219
fridge, left by her granddaughter: government

47
00:01:50,219 --> 00:01:51,574
agencies don't call.

48
00:01:51,574 --> 00:01:52,090
Hang up.

49
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:54,759
Number four: romance scams.

50
00:01:54,759 --> 00:01:57,437
Nine hundred twenty-nine million dollars.

51
00:01:57,977 --> 00:02:00,945
Marisol is fifty-eight, a nurse, divorced.

52
00:02:00,945 --> 00:02:04,188
On a dating app, she meets Daniel, an engineer

53
00:02:04,188 --> 00:02:06,396
working on an oil rig overseas.

54
00:02:06,396 --> 00:02:08,122
He writes every morning.

55
00:02:08,122 --> 00:02:09,778
He remembers her shifts.

56
00:02:10,318 --> 00:02:11,834
They never video chat.

57
00:02:11,834 --> 00:02:14,536
The camera on the rig is 'broken.' After

58
00:02:14,536 --> 00:02:17,370
three months, Daniel has an emergency: his

59
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:20,138
bank account is frozen, and he needs help

60
00:02:20,138 --> 00:02:21,523
paying a customs fee.

61
00:02:22,063 --> 00:02:24,371
The scammer isn't selling a product.

62
00:02:24,371 --> 00:02:26,180
He's selling a relationship.

63
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:28,800
And the longer it lasts, the harder it is

64
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:29,423
to say no.

65
00:02:29,963 --> 00:02:33,108
Romance scammers often build these relationships

66
00:02:33,108 --> 00:02:35,867
for months. They learn your schedule, your

67
00:02:35,867 --> 00:02:37,150
fears, your dreams.

68
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:39,910
By the time they ask for money, it doesn't

69
00:02:39,910 --> 00:02:41,771
feel like a stranger asking.

70
00:02:41,771 --> 00:02:43,760
It feels like someone you love.

71
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:46,682
Marisol sent fifteen hundred dollars.

72
00:02:46,682 --> 00:02:49,440
Then four thousand more, for a flight home.

73
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:51,070
The flight never happened.

74
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:54,307
The red flag: someone you've never met in

75
00:02:54,307 --> 00:02:57,197
person, who always has a reason not to video

76
00:02:57,197 --> 00:02:58,995
call, and then needs money.

77
00:02:58,995 --> 00:02:59,701
Every time.

78
00:03:00,541 --> 00:03:02,586
Number three: fake tech support.

79
00:03:02,586 --> 00:03:04,506
Two point one billion dollars.

80
00:03:04,506 --> 00:03:07,108
That number more than doubled in just two

81
00:03:07,108 --> 00:03:07,480
years.

82
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:09,673
Harold is seventy-four.

83
00:03:09,673 --> 00:03:12,704
One evening, his computer screen turns red.

84
00:03:12,704 --> 00:03:13,806
An alarm sound.

85
00:03:13,806 --> 00:03:16,492
A message: 'Your computer is infected.

86
00:03:16,492 --> 00:03:17,870
Do not turn it off.

87
00:03:17,870 --> 00:03:20,763
Call support now.' There's a phone number.

88
00:03:21,303 --> 00:03:23,875
The friendly 'technician' asks for remote

89
00:03:23,875 --> 00:03:24,978
access to fix it.

90
00:03:24,978 --> 00:03:27,550
Then he says hackers are in Harold's bank

91
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:30,000
account, and he needs to move the money

92
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:30,979
to keep it safe.

93
00:03:31,519 --> 00:03:33,891
Notice the pattern: fear, then a helper,

94
00:03:33,891 --> 00:03:35,974
then a 'safe' place for your money.

95
00:03:35,974 --> 00:03:37,131
You'll see it again.

96
00:03:37,671 --> 00:03:40,427
Some versions start with a phone call instead

97
00:03:40,427 --> 00:03:42,823
of a pop-up. Others hide in fake ads at

98
00:03:42,823 --> 00:03:45,460
the top of search results, so when you look

99
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,916
for your bank's support number, you call

100
00:03:47,916 --> 00:03:49,114
the scammer instead.

101
00:03:49,654 --> 00:03:52,120
Harold lost forty-two thousand dollars.

102
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,648
He said the worst part wasn't the money.

103
00:03:54,648 --> 00:03:56,375
It was how polite they were.

104
00:03:56,915 --> 00:03:59,566
The red flag: real tech companies never

105
00:03:59,566 --> 00:04:02,086
put phone numbers in warning pop-ups.

106
00:04:02,086 --> 00:04:05,003
Close the browser, or restart the computer.

107
00:04:05,003 --> 00:04:07,654
And never give a stranger remote access.

108
00:04:08,494 --> 00:04:10,962
Number two: business email compromise.

109
00:04:10,962 --> 00:04:13,746
Three point zero five billion dollars, from

110
00:04:13,746 --> 00:04:16,530
fewer than twenty-five thousand complaints.

111
00:04:16,530 --> 00:04:19,503
That's more than a hundred and twenty thousand

112
00:04:19,503 --> 00:04:20,895
dollars per complaint.

113
00:04:21,435 --> 00:04:24,458
Priya is the bookkeeper at a small construction

114
00:04:24,458 --> 00:04:27,418
company. On Friday afternoon, an email arrives

115
00:04:27,418 --> 00:04:29,937
from a supplier they've worked with for

116
00:04:29,937 --> 00:04:32,393
years. 'Our bank details have changed.

117
00:04:32,393 --> 00:04:35,101
Please send this week's payment to the new

118
00:04:35,101 --> 00:04:35,668
account.'

119
00:04:36,208 --> 00:04:38,122
Same logo. Same signature.

120
00:04:38,122 --> 00:04:39,539
Same writing style.

121
00:04:39,539 --> 00:04:42,870
But the email address is one letter different.

122
00:04:42,870 --> 00:04:45,846
Priya sends a hundred and eighty thousand

123
00:04:45,846 --> 00:04:46,413
dollars.

124
00:04:46,953 --> 00:04:49,648
This one hits businesses, but also home

125
00:04:49,648 --> 00:04:52,881
buyers. Scammers send fake closing instructions

126
00:04:52,881 --> 00:04:54,902
right before a house purchase.

127
00:04:55,442 --> 00:04:58,164
It's the most profitable scam per victim,

128
00:04:58,164 --> 00:05:00,951
because the payments are big and they look

129
00:05:00,951 --> 00:05:03,673
routine. Scammers often break into a real

130
00:05:03,673 --> 00:05:06,396
email account first, and quietly read the

131
00:05:06,396 --> 00:05:09,118
invoices for weeks, waiting for the right

132
00:05:09,118 --> 00:05:09,571
moment.

133
00:05:10,111 --> 00:05:13,241
The red flag: any email that changes payment

134
00:05:13,241 --> 00:05:16,092
details. Always call to confirm, using a

135
00:05:16,092 --> 00:05:17,830
number you already have.

136
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:19,569
Not the one in the email.

137
00:05:20,109 --> 00:05:22,813
Priya's company got some of the money back,

138
00:05:22,813 --> 00:05:25,333
only because they called the bank within

139
00:05:25,333 --> 00:05:27,914
hours. With wire fraud, every hour counts.

140
00:05:28,954 --> 00:05:31,309
And number one: investment fraud.

141
00:05:31,309 --> 00:05:33,941
Eight point six five billion dollars.

142
00:05:33,941 --> 00:05:36,781
More than forty percent of all the money

143
00:05:36,781 --> 00:05:38,512
reported lost to the FBI.

144
00:05:39,052 --> 00:05:41,241
Tom and Ann are retired teachers.

145
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:43,882
It starts with a friendly message from a

146
00:05:43,882 --> 00:05:46,522
stranger who says she made money trading

147
00:05:46,522 --> 00:05:49,033
crypto. She never asks them for money.

148
00:05:49,033 --> 00:05:50,836
She just shares her success.

149
00:05:51,376 --> 00:05:52,744
She recommends an app.

150
00:05:52,744 --> 00:05:54,469
They try a thousand dollars.

151
00:05:54,469 --> 00:05:55,599
The balance grows.

152
00:05:55,599 --> 00:05:58,097
They even withdraw a little, and it works.

153
00:05:58,637 --> 00:06:01,178
So they put in their retirement savings.

154
00:06:01,178 --> 00:06:03,037
Two hundred thousand dollars.

155
00:06:03,037 --> 00:06:05,641
And when they try to take it out, the app

156
00:06:05,641 --> 00:06:08,120
asks for a 'tax.' Then a 'fee.' Then it

157
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:09,112
stops answering.

158
00:06:09,652 --> 00:06:12,628
The balance on the screen was never real.

159
00:06:12,628 --> 00:06:15,746
And the FBI says crypto-related scams alone

160
00:06:15,746 --> 00:06:18,652
cost Americans eleven point four billion

161
00:06:18,652 --> 00:06:19,998
dollars last year.

162
00:06:19,998 --> 00:06:23,329
People over sixty lost four point four billion

163
00:06:23,329 --> 00:06:23,754
of it.

164
00:06:24,294 --> 00:06:26,947
Investigators call many of these schemes

165
00:06:26,947 --> 00:06:29,730
pig butchering, because the scammers spend

166
00:06:29,730 --> 00:06:32,448
weeks or months building trust before the

167
00:06:32,448 --> 00:06:34,001
final, biggest payment.

168
00:06:34,001 --> 00:06:36,913
And many of the people typing those friendly

169
00:06:36,913 --> 00:06:39,825
messages are themselves trafficking victims,

170
00:06:39,825 --> 00:06:42,672
forced to run scams from compounds overseas.

171
00:06:43,212 --> 00:06:46,116
The red flag: guaranteed returns, a stranger

172
00:06:46,116 --> 00:06:48,698
as your guide, and fees to get your own

173
00:06:48,698 --> 00:06:51,537
money back. If you have to pay to withdraw,

174
00:06:51,537 --> 00:06:53,215
the money is already gone.

175
00:06:53,755 --> 00:06:56,258
Tom and Ann finally told their daughter.

176
00:06:56,258 --> 00:06:58,699
She helped them report it to their bank

177
00:06:58,699 --> 00:07:01,324
and the FBI. They got back almost nothing.

178
00:07:01,324 --> 00:07:04,132
But they stopped before they borrowed against

179
00:07:04,132 --> 00:07:04,864
their house.

180
00:07:05,804 --> 00:07:08,336
Five scams. Five different stories.

181
00:07:08,336 --> 00:07:11,711
But look at what they share: a sudden emergency

182
00:07:11,711 --> 00:07:14,734
or a dream, a stranger you trust, secrecy,

183
00:07:14,734 --> 00:07:16,984
and money that has to move fast.

184
00:07:17,524 --> 00:07:20,338
None of these scams need you to be careless.

185
00:07:20,338 --> 00:07:23,026
They need you to be human: scared, lonely,

186
00:07:23,026 --> 00:07:24,152
busy, or hopeful.

187
00:07:24,152 --> 00:07:26,653
That's why the best defense isn't being

188
00:07:26,653 --> 00:07:29,217
smart. It's one rule you never break: no

189
00:07:29,217 --> 00:07:32,093
money moves until you've checked with someone

190
00:07:32,093 --> 00:07:32,719
you trust.

191
00:07:33,259 --> 00:07:36,091
If you spot even one of those, stop.

192
00:07:36,091 --> 00:07:38,617
Hang up. Call someone you trust.

193
00:07:38,617 --> 00:07:40,530
And report it at ic3.gov.

194
00:07:41,070 --> 00:07:44,015
People over sixty reported seven point seven

195
00:07:44,015 --> 00:07:46,436
billion dollars in losses last year.

196
00:07:46,436 --> 00:07:49,053
Send this video to someone who needs to

197
00:07:49,053 --> 00:07:49,512
see it.

198
00:07:50,052 --> 00:07:52,956
Next time on Scam Anatomy: we go deep into

199
00:07:52,956 --> 00:07:55,861
number one, and follow one investment scam

200
00:07:55,861 --> 00:07:58,563
from the first text to the last dollar.

201
00:07:58,563 --> 00:08:00,724
Subscribe, so you see it coming.
